WEBVTT

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:06.160
This is the Singularity Daily, for Sunday, November 22.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.930
Here are the last 24 hours in superintelligence, in 3 minutes.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.880
Show your work. Hours after Claude's claimed Riemann proof,

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.030
a Stanford professor posted an all-human rival.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:26.490
Within about eight hours, people using AI models had flagged problems in it.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:32.750
Printed out,

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:38.060
the Anthropic mathematician's proof with Claude would top 250,000 pages.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:40.090
This week,

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:45.640
he and an NYU professor also posted an AI-aided proof about how fluids swirl.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:47.830
Over in Korea,

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:52.790
two AI models scored a perfect 450 on this year's college entrance exam.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.580
After more than 100 cheating cases,

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.880
Seoul National University will hand out its own pens.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.160
Here in Australia, the ABC found cheating kits online,

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:06.790
button cameras and tiny earpieces, for a few hundred dollars.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.820
Canberra's eyeing AI shopping agents too.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:14.170
Andrew Leigh doesn't want Australians mucked around by surveillance pricing.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.020
Oh, and in LA,

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:21.580
a $30 million mansion will take Anthropic or OpenAI shares instead of cash.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.800
And on eBay, an 'employees-only' Anthropic hoodie is listed at $1,800.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:30.760
Meanwhile,

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:36.320
a tech writer squeezed a tiny ChatGPT onto a 35-year-old Super Nintendo.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.030
It knows about 8,000 words, often gets things wrong,

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.060
and AI agents wrote its code.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:51.040
The catch? At UN killer-robot talks, 128 countries agreed only to keep talking.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:55.400
Human Rights Watch says the technology is racing ahead.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.370
So... is it so over?

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.450
We're so back!

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:02.600
In Singapore,

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.650
a robot face with hundreds of expressions will help trainees practise tough conversations.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:09.510
Why not a screen?

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:14.430
"A physical face creates real emotional pressure," says its Singapore backer.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.860
And this week in Santa Monica,

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.960
Zoox robotaxis with no steering wheel started giving staff rides.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.260
Proofs, exams, robots: the same rule as school. Show your work.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.030
Day after day, the curve keeps bending upward.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.780
Somehow, the future keeps landing early.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.370
That's one day of news. Imagine what a year looks like.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.100
This has been the Singularity Daily.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:50.120
Same time tomorrow?

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.010
Want the whole month? Hear more on November 27.
